    "q-93289": {
      "dom_id": "step-q93289",
      "name": "Question 16",
      "problem": "The following table shows Herbert's height and the length of his shadow, in centimeters. \n\n| Height | Shadow |\n| --- | --- |\n| $197$ | $162$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nThe ratio of the height of the object to the length of its ​shadow is the same for different objects at the same moment of time. By rounding the given data to the nearest ten, approximate the length of the shadow cast by Regina at the same time, if her height is $150$ centimeters.",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's round Herbert's height and the length of his shadow:\n\n- Height: $197⟶200$\n\n- Shadow: $162⟶160$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the height to the length of the ​shadow (expressed as a fraction) is approximately\n\n$$\n\\frac{200}{160}.\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the height and the denominator represents the length of the ​shadow.\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $10$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{200÷10}{160÷10}=\\frac{20}{16}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{20÷4}{16÷4}=\\frac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can make a numerator of $150$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $30$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5\\times 30}{4\\times 30}=\\frac{150}{120}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the shadow cast by Regina is approximately $120$ centimeters.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
